439:
Any permit or license issued pursuant to this article for the operation of a flea market may be revoked by the mayor and alderman, after notice and hearing, for any of the following causes:
(1) Any fraud, misrepresentation or false statement contained in the application for permit or license;
(2) Any fraud, misrepresentation or false statement made by the permittee in connection with the selling of goods, wares or merchandise;
(3) Any violation by the permittee of this article;
(4) The conviction of the permittee of any felony, or of a misdemeanor involving moral turpitude;
(5) The conducting of the business permitted under this article by the permittee in an unlawful manner or in such a manner as to constitute a menace to the health, safety or general welfare of the public.
Notice of hearing for revocation of a permit issued under this article shall be given in writing, setting forth specifically the grounds of the complaint and the time and place of the hearing. Such notice shall be served on the permittee by handing the same personally to the person in charge of the permitted business, or by mailing the same, postage prepaid, to the permittee at his or her last known address, at least five days prior to the date set for hearing.
The giving of such notice shall suspend the permit pending the outcome of the hearing, and business operations under the permit shall cease.
(Ord. No. 2009-0223, § 1, 2-23-09)

478:
Before operating a pawnshop or becoming an employee of a pawnshop, a person or entity must first file an application with the police department for a license to operate or a permit to be employed in the pawnshop, pursuant to the following:
(a) The application shall be made on an annual basis;
(b) No license or permit shall be issued until a fee in an amount established by action of the mayor and council, a copy of which is on file in the office of the city clerk, is paid to the city;
(c) The application shall state the physical address of the pawnshop;
(d) The application shall contain the full name, street address, mailing address, phone number, facsimile number, email address, Social Security number, and date of birth and of any employee, owner, or pawnbroker;
(e) The chief of police or any other officer of the city designated by the city manager shall investigate each applicant for such license or permit and, in the case of a license application, shall report to the city manager whether such applicant is a person of good character and whether the applicant has a criminal conviction.
(Ord. No. 2012-0924, § 1, 9-24-12)

